The '''2026 NCAA Division I men's basketball tournament''' was an event involving 68 teams playing in a [[single-elimination tournament]] to determine the [[NCAA Division I]] men's [[college basketball]] national champion for the [[2025–26 NCAA Division I men's basketball season|2025–26 season]]. The 87th edition of the tournament began on March 17 and concluded with the [[2026 NCAA Division I men's basketball championship game|championship game]] on April 6, at [[Lucas Oil Stadium]] in [[Indianapolis]]. The Michigan Wolverines won their second title, and the first under Dusty May. It was Michigan’s first national championship since [[1989 NCAA Division I men's basketball championship game|1989]], ending a streak of four straight title game defeats (two of which were vacated by the NCAA) for the Wolverines.

[[2026 Atlantic Sun men's basketball tournament|Atlantic Sun]] champion [[2025–26 Queens Royals men's basketball team|Queens]] and [[2026 WAC men's basketball tournament|Western Athletic Conference]] (WAC) champion [[2025–26 California Baptist Lancers men's basketball team|California Baptist]] made their tournament debuts. Queens qualified in its first year of eligibility, becoming only the fifth school since 1972 to achieve the feat. 

[[2026 Ohio Valley Conference men's basketball tournament|Ohio Valley]] champion [[2025–26 Tennessee State Tigers basketball team|Tennessee State]] made its third-ever appearance, its first since [[1994 NCAA Division I men's basketball tournament|1994]], while [[2026 Big Sky Conference men's basketball tournament|Big Sky]] champion [[2025–26 Idaho Vandals men's basketball team|Idaho]] made its first appearance since [[1990 NCAA Division I men's basketball tournament|1990]]. [[2026 CAA men's basketball tournament|Coastal Athletic]] champion [[2025–26 Hofstra Pride men's basketball team|Hofstra]] made its first appearance since [[2001 NCAA Division I men's basketball tournament|2001]].{{efn|Hofstra had received an automatic bid to the [[2020 NCAA Division I men's basketball tournament|2020 tournament]] for winning that year's [[2020 CAA men's basketball tournament|CAA tournament]] before the former was cancelled due to the [[Impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on basketball#College|COVID-19 pandemic]].}} [[2026 MAC men's basketball tournament|Mid-American Conference]] (MAC) champion [[2025–26 Akron Zips men's basketball team|Akron]] and [[2026 Southland Conference men's basketball tournament|Southland]] champion [[2025–26 McNeese Cowboys basketball team|McNeese]] each made their third consecutive tournament appearance.

For the first time since [[2022 NCAA Division I men's basketball tournament|2022]] there was a [[Philadelphia Big 5]] school in the tournament, with both [[2025–26 Villanova Wildcats men's basketball team|Villanova]] and [[2025–26 Penn Quakers men's basketball team|Penn]] qualifying. Teams from 33 U.S. states and the District of Columbia participated.

For the second consecutive season, all top four seeds advanced to the second round. This was the first time since the tournament expanded to 64 teams in 1985 that this had happened in consecutive seasons.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.on3.com/teams/kentucky-wildcats/news/ncaa-tournament-friday-chalk-betting-favorites/ |title=Friday was absolute chalk, making NCAA Tournament history |first=Adam |last=Luckett |website=[[On3.com]] |publisher=Kentucky Sports Radio |date=March 21, 2026 |access-date=March 21, 2026}}</ref> The biggest upset in the first round was No. 5 seed [[2025–26 Wisconsin Badgers men's basketball team|Wisconsin]] losing to No. 12 seed [[2025-26 High Point Panthers men's basketball team|High Point]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.espn.com/mens-college-basketball/recap/_/gameId/401856480|title=High Point stuns Wisconsin 83-82 in March Madness on Chase Johnston's first 2-point basket of season|website=[[ESPN]]|agency=[[Associated Press]]|date=March 19, 2026|access-date=March 20, 2026}}</ref> [[2025–26 Nebraska Cornhuskers men's basketball team|Nebraska]], High Point, [[2025–26 Howard Bison men's basketball team|Howard]], and [[2025–26 Prairie View A&M Panthers basketball team|Prairie View A&M]] each earned their first ever tournament wins, although Howard and Prairie View A&M accomplished this in the First Four. Nebraska had previously been the only [[Power conferences#Outside of football|power conference]] team to have never won an NCAA tournament game.<ref>{{cite web|last=McKenna|first=Dave|url=https://defector.com/howards-first-ncaa-tournament-win-was-a-long-time-coming|title=Howard’s First NCAA Tournament Win Was A Long Time Coming|website=[[Defector Media]]|date=March 18, 2026|access-date=March 20, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|last=Bromberg|first=Nick|url=https://sports.yahoo.com/mens-college-basketball/breaking-news/article/no-4-nebraska-gets-its-first-mens-ncaa-tournament-win-in-school-history-with-victory-over-no-13-troy-183858387.html|title=No. 4 Nebraska gets its first men's NCAA tournament win in school history with victory over No. 13 Troy|website=[[Yahoo Sports]]|date=March 20, 2026|access-date=March 20, 2026}}</ref> The average margin of victory in the first round was 17.4 points, the highest since the tournament expanded in 1985. This, coupled with higher seeds' tendency to win during the 2025 tournament, has led to a belief that mid-major programs are unable to compete with major conference programs in the [[Student athlete compensation|NIL]] and transfer portal era.<ref>{{cite web|last=Rosvoglou|first=Chris|url=https://sports.yahoo.com/articles/concerning-stat-proves-ncaa-tournament-122103249.html|title=Concerning Stat Proves NCAA Tournament Has Lost Its Magic|website=[[Yahoo Sports]]|date=March 21, 2026|access-date=March 21, 2026}}</ref> However, No. 1 overall seed [[2025–26 Duke Blue Devils men's basketball team|Duke]] did get a challenge from No. 16 seed [[2025–26 Siena Saints men's basketball team|Siena]], trailing by 13 points before recovering to win the game by six points.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.espn.com/mens-college-basketball/recap/_/gameId/401856478|title=No. 1 overall seed Duke survives 16-seed Siena, rallies for 71-65 win in 1st round of March Madness|website=ESPN|agency=Associated Press|date=March 19, 2026|access-date=March 21, 2026}}</ref> Additionally, for the 18th straight year, a double digit seed made the Sweet 16, although for the third consecutive year no 12-seed or lower team made the Sweet 16, and for the fourth consecutive year only one double digit seed made the Sweet 16.<ref>{{cite web|last=Lipscomb|first=Keith|url=https://www.espn.com/mens-college-basketball/story/_/id/48176244/2026-march-madness-bracket-facts-mens-ncaa-tournament|title=2026 March Madness bracket facts for men's NCAA tournament|website=ESPN|date=March 15, 2026|access-date=March 22, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Feldscher|first=Larry|url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/03/21/sport/march-madness-second-round-saturday|title=Texas pulls off an upset to be the first double-digit seed to go to the Sweet 16. Here’s what to know from March Madness|work=[[CNN]]|date=March 21, 2026|access-date=March 22, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Armour|first=Nancy|url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/ncaab/2026/03/22/march-madness-lacks-underdogs-upsets-ncaa-tournament-bracket-chalk-top-seeds-sweet-16/89275765007/|title=Iowa brings much-needed chaos to NCAA Tournament with upset of Florida|work=[[USA Today]]|date=March 22, 2026|access-date=March 22, 2026}}</ref> For the second straight season, no mid-major teams made the Sweet 16.<ref>{{cite news|last1=Burge|first1=Dave|last2=Morgan|first2=Andy|url=https://www.ktsm.com/sports/college-basketball-analyst-to-talk-march-madness-utep-hoops/|title=Stealing Cinderella: Mid-Majors absent from Sweet 16 of March Madness|website=[[KTSM-TV|KTSM]]|date=March 23, 2026|access-date=March 24, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Marshall|first=John|url=https://cdispatch.com/sports/bracket-reset-ncaa-tournament-down-to-a-sweet-16-of-high-majors/|title=Bracket Reset: NCAA Tournament down to a Sweet 16 of high-majors|work=[[The Commercial Dispatch]]|date=March 24, 2026|access-date=March 24, 2026}}</ref>

The Final Four comprised No. 1 seed Michigan, No. 1 seed Arizona, No. 2 seed Connecticut, and No. 3 seed Illinois. While Illinois was the lowest numbered seed to make the Final Four in two years, the Final Four was noted for not having a real Cinderella story.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.mynbc5.com/article/mens-final-four-uconn-illinois-arizona-michigan/70877352|title=Men's Final Four is set|work=[[WPTZ]]|agency=Associated Press|date=March 30, 2026|access-date=March 31, 2026}}</ref>

This was the final tournament played under the 68-team format, as the NCAA voted to expand the tournament to 76 teams for the [[2027 NCAA Division I men's basketball tournament|2027 edition]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Borzello |first=Jeff |date=2026-05-07 |title=NCAA men's, women's basketball tournaments expand to 76 teams |url=https://www.espn.com/mens-college-basketball/story/_/id/48704864/ncaa-mens-women-basketball-tournaments-expand-76-teams |access-date=2026-05-07 |website=ESPN |language=en}}</ref>

== Tournament procedure ==
{{details|NCAA Division I men's basketball tournament#Format}}
	
A total of 68 teams entered the 2026 tournament. Thirty-one automatic bids were awarded to each program that won its conference's tournament. The remaining 37 teams received at-large bids, with selections extended by the [[NCAA basketball tournament selection process|NCAA selection committee]] on Selection Sunday (March 15). The selection committee also [[Seeding (sports)|seeded]] the entire field from 1 to 68.

Eight teams (the four-lowest seeded automatic qualifiers and the four lowest-seeded at large-teams) played in the First Four. The winners of these games advanced to the main tournament bracket.

