==Media coverage==
===Television===
{{main|NCAA March Madness (TV program)}}

[[CBS Sports]] and [[TNT Sports (United States)|TNT Sports]] had US television rights to the tournament.<ref name="wp=2032extend">{{cite news|last=Bonesteel|first=Matt|title=CBS And Turner lock down NCAA Tournament Through 2032|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/|newspaper=[[The Washington Post]]|date=April 12, 2016|accessdate=April 12, 2016}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=CBS Sports and TNT Sports Announce 2026 NCAA Division I Men’s Basketball Championship Commentator Teams |url=https://www.ncaa.com/news/basketball-men/article/2026-03-04/cbs-sports-and-tnt-sports-announce-2026-ncaa-division-i-mens-basketball |access-date=2026-03-04 |publisher=[[National Collegiate Athletic Association]] |date=March 4, 2026}}</ref> As part of a cycle that began in 2016, [[TBS (American TV channel)|TBS]] televised the 2026 Final Four and the National Championship Game.

In November 2025, [[College Basketball on TNT Sports|TNT Sports]] and [[ESPN]] announced an agreement to allow ESPN's [[Dick Vitale]] to call a First Four game on truTV alongside TNT Sports' [[Charles Barkley]]. In exchange, Vitale and Barkley called a December 13, 2025 regular season game on ESPN between Indiana and Kentucky.<ref>{{cite news|last=Bradley|first=Colin|title=Legendary hoops icons Dick Vitale and Charles Barkley team up for two must-see college basketball telecasts this season|url=https://espnpressroom.com/us/press-releases/2025/12/legendary-hoops-icons-dick-vitale-and-charles-barkley-team-up-for-two-must-see-college-basketball-telecasts-this-season/|newspaper=ESPN Press Room|date=December 1, 2025|accessdate=December 2, 2025}}</ref>

''[[CBS Mornings]]'' co-host and ''[[The NFL Today]]'' analyst [[Nate Burleson]] replaced [[Ernie Johnson Jr.|Ernie Johnson]] for the first two weeks of the tournament, as Johnson announced his semi-retirement from hosting March Madness to focus on his increasing ''[[Inside the NBA]]'' duties for [[NBA on ESPN|ESPN]] and [[NBA on ABC|ABC]] under a sub-licensing agreement from TNT Sports. Johnson continued to host the Final Four and National Championship Game.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lerner|first=Drew|date=February 19, 2026|title=Nate Burleson to host March Madness coverage for CBS Sports|url=https://awfulannouncing.com/cbs/nate-burleson-host-march-madness-coverage.html |access-date=February 19, 2026|work=[[Awful Announcing]]}}</ref> 

